; restpace.eval - rest at its REAL 60x (code-review batch 35: C64, C48).
; tools\AITest.py runs this and JUDGES it; on its own it only reports. Every
; section header is `=== <name> ===`, as in ai.eval.
;
; `step` cannot see rest's multiplier: it feeds the world fixed ticks of sim
; time and never asks how much a frame is worth. `frames` runs PLAY frames
; instead - a sixtieth of a second each through the one multiplier site, into
; the world's Update - so a resting frame here hands the world a second, as it
; does in play. `frames ... whole` takes each frame's second as ONE step, the
; world's fixed ticks bypassed: what every resting frame did before C64.
;
; Lockstep at timescale 0, so nothing moves between lines.
logecho on
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on
seed 5

; --- C64: a chaser, awake and at rest ------------------------------------------
; The same two seconds three times: a skeleton six squares down a corridor,
; facing the party - its aggro, so its first think sees the party and every
; one after chases - walks at it while the party stands still. Awake, then
; resting at 60x, then resting with each frame's second taken as one step. The
; judge compares the squares it walked and the AI's thinks (the `frames` line's
; `AI thinks`: lockstep's inline computes, all four buckets, inside those
; frames alone) per simulated second. The party is wounded each time, so a
; rest has something to gain and does not end by itself, and `lockstep on`
; before the awake window zeroes the bucket clocks exactly as `rest on` does.
; Two seconds because it is a step short of the party by then.
;   arena corridor 24: floor 2..25 x 12; the party at 3,12, the skeleton at 9.
echo === pace-awake ===
goto eval_arena
arena corridor 24 1
heal
setpool all health 20
tp 3 12
face e
spawn skeleton 9 12 west 1 up
lockstep on
monsters
frames 120 60
monsters
rest

echo === pace-rest ===
arena corridor 24 1
heal
setpool all health 20
tp 3 12
face e
spawn skeleton 9 12 west 1 up
rest on
monsters
frames 2 60
monsters
rest
rest off

; The defect itself, kept as the measurement's own proof that it can see one.
echo === pace-whole ===
arena corridor 24 1
heal
setpool all health 20
tp 3 12
face e
spawn skeleton 9 12 west 1 up
rest on
monsters
frames 2 60 whole
monsters
rest
rest off

; The cap. A frame at 20 a second hands a resting world three seconds; it takes
; 90 ticks (a second and a half) and DROPS the rest, so it leaves the next frame
; nothing owed. Read off that frame's own `owed`, never a second `frames` line:
; the runner's frames between two lines are zero-dt whole steps at timescale 0,
; and a whole step clears the carry, so the next line would owe nothing however
; the cap behaved. A frame at 70 a second hands the world 6/7 s - 51 ticks and
; three sevenths of one - and must leave that part OWED, which is what shows the
; readout sees the carry at all.
echo === cap ===
arena corridor 24 1
heal
setpool all health 20
tp 3 12
rest on
frames 1 20
frames 1 70
rest
rest off

; --- C48: one 60x frame as ONE step ---------------------------------------------
; crypt1, the party resting at 5,4 facing east. Two shots loosed from 6,4 west
; at it: a second's flight is 6 m, which from the next square's centre ends at
; 4,4 - beyond the party. Only a flight stepped half a square at a time meets
; the party's square on the way, and lands, and ends the rest as `attacked`.
; Two shots so a fumble (a 1-in-20 automatic miss) cannot leave the party
; unhit; the seed fixes which faces come up.
echo === rest-shot ===
reset
goto crypt1
heal
setpool all health 20
tp 5 4
face e
rest on
tally reset
shot 6 4 w 2
shot 6 4 w 2
frames 1 60 whole
rest
tally

; A shot loosed from 10,4 east: the wall at 11,4 is one square thick, with the
; room's outer corridor open at 12,4 beyond it, and a second's flight would end
; there. Stepped, it stops IN the wall (`expat=11,4`) and nothing flies on.
echo === wall-shot ===
heal
setpool all health 20
tp 5 4
face e
rest on
tally reset
shot 10 4 e 2
frames 1 60 whole
rest
tally

echo === end ===
state
